00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.706
Now let's directly jump
into the video on use cases.

00:01:28.706 --> 00:01:32.330
In this section we will
focus on use cases.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.320
We will discuss different
kinds of applications

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.710
and things you can actually do and build

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.813
with blockchains, and explain
how this will be useful

00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:44.823
for the industry, for people
and for businesses in general.

00:01:45.736 --> 00:01:48.770
Of course, blockchains need to enable

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.540
tangible use cases to be successful.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.900
Blockchain is an exciting
and interesting technology,

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.300
but if it would turn out
that we cannot really

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.830
do something with that,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.990
this technology will remain unimportant.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.640
And not be used, and not
be relevant in the future.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.863
So what can we actually
do with blockchains?

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.034
In the book "The Business Blockchain"

00:02:10.034 --> 00:02:13.180
William Mougayar introduces a framework

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.540
which I find very interesting and useful

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.510
in order to discuss this topic.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.970
It is the ATOMIC framework.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.350
And ATOMIC can be used as a mnemonic

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.180
in order to help you memorize

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.620
some key blockchain use
case areas that we will now

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.380
explore in more detail
in the later lectures.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.160
So the first letter of
ATOMIC A stands for assets.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.200
So blockchain can help us
secure assets on a blockchain.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.080
T stands for trust, blockchain
is a trustless technology,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.850
it doesn't require that the users

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.100
of blockchains trust each other.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.960
O stands for ownership.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.654
It can facilitate proof of
ownership, proof of identity,

00:02:52.654 --> 00:02:56.443
and there are use cases that
really revolve around that.

00:02:56.443 --> 00:02:57.276
M for money.

00:02:57.276 --> 00:03:00.400
Of course cryptocurrencies
are the most prevalent

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.830
use case of blockchain, we are
most familiar with that one.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.430
I for identity.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.890
Proof of identity is important as well.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.440
With blockchains you
can legitimate yourself

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.860
as the person that you actually are.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.430
And C, contracts.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.640
Smart contracts are a
huge topic for itself,

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.260
probably you could make whole courses

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.910
about the topic as well,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.730
we will discuss that as
well in the later lectures

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:24.833
how this plays along.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.460
One important note that I wanna make

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.890
is that, of course, many use cases

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.050
will touch different ones of these areas.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:35.800
So a use case can be all about
assets and money as well.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.034
And probably bitcoin
would be a good example.

00:03:38.034 --> 00:03:41.300
Bitcoin is a way of payment,
you can use it as money,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.380
but it's also considered an asset.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.900
And, of course, it's
also kind of trustless,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.940
because the uses of the bitcoin network,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.890
you don't need to trust each other.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.150
So hope you understand that many use cases

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.312
touch different aspects
of this ATOMIC framework.

00:03:55.312 --> 00:03:59.200
Nevertheless, I think this is
a useful way to conceptualize

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.230
the different advantages and use cases

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.560
that blockchain can bring to the table.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.370
In this lecture we will cover
the first group of use cases,

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.520
and this is the group of
use cases related to assets.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.430
For assets, blockchains
provide a whole set

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.810
of distinctive advantages
compared to prior use cases

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.900
and solutions that existed.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.510
With the help of a blockchain
digital assets can be created

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.433
and safely stored on the blockchain.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.410
The assets can also be
moved among different users

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.640
of the blockchain in order
to facilitate the transfer

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.350
or the change of ownership of an asset.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:40.000
In addition, prior intermediaries
can be disintermediated.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.290
Maybe in the past there was a bank,

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:45.150
or some kind of other
clearance institution

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.120
that helped you to create
and move these assets.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.290
These intermediaries are
no longer required anymore.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.319
And this will help you with
transaction times as well.

00:04:54.319 --> 00:04:55.960
To make a bank transaction for instance,

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.410
in the future a bank
usually took several days

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.290
in order to be able to
clear the transactions.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.840
On a blockchain this happens
virtually immediately

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.880
because the blockchain will not require

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.793
any kind of intermediaries like that.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.330
From this analysis, a whole
set of use cases arises.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.810
And here are a few examples,

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.170
this is not necessarily
an exhaustive list,

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.230
but I hope this gives
you a first understanding

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:21.740
of what is possible.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:24.390
The first group of examples
is a group of examples

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.040
related to Colored Coins.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.500
And Colored Coins are
representation of real world assets

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.350
in the digital sphere that
facilitate the handling

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.150
and trade of these assets.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:38.448
So imagine you have a car,
to make a simple example,

00:05:38.448 --> 00:05:40.731
you have bought a car, you own a car,

00:05:40.731 --> 00:05:43.270
and now you want to secure your ownership

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.390
of the car in the blockchain.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.300
And maybe in the future transfer
the ownership of the car

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.340
without a need to go through
some kind of intermediary,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.700
to some kind of legal scholar to help you

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.150
facilitate and set up all the paperwork.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.161
Now you could use a blockchain
in order to create a record

00:05:59.161 --> 00:06:02.540
of you owning this car, in the blockchain,

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.880
that kind of contains
all of the information

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:05.827
that is required.

00:06:05.827 --> 00:06:09.180
And this record of the asset
that you actually possess

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.630
is called a Colored Coin.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:14.170
So kind of a digital coin that is colored

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:17.650
with all of the information
of the actual physical asset.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.550
And now in the future, if you
would like to trade the car,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.320
instead of meeting somebody
and giving them the car

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.990
you could just reassign the coin

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.100
that represents your car on the blockchain

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.940
to another person as a sign
of transfer of ownership.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.240
So this is the whole
concept of Colored Coins.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.040
Somehow related to that is the concept

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.020
of digital collectibles,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.120
so you could create
collectibles on blockchain.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.740
And due to the blockchain
these collectibles

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.520
will be protected, that you own them,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.460
this would be clear from the blockchain.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.200
And also the rarity and
other relevant properties

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.350
of the collectible will be protected,

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.470
because there's no central institution

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:00.178
that can just release more
items of the collectible.

00:07:00.178 --> 00:07:04.300
So think of collectible cars,
or maybe collectible coins,

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.150
or any kind of other collectible assets

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.184
that right now, today,
exist in the physical world.

00:07:09.184 --> 00:07:11.840
You're often at risk that
the central institution

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.150
that gives out these
collectibles just recreates them,

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.380
reprints them, and increases the quantity

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:18.763
of these collectibles.

00:07:18.763 --> 00:07:21.050
And this is not possible if you use

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.007
collectibles on a blockchain

00:07:22.007 --> 00:07:25.050
and the blockchain is
set up in the right way.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.850
And then online gaming,
the same is true there.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.470
Imagine you're playing in online game

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.110
where you maybe even spent the money

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.140
in order to advance your character,

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.220
or you have bought certain items that help

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.410
your character progress in the game,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.950
now you're always at risk
that the central institution

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.570
that leads the game, the game publisher,

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.340
or the game creator, suddenly
changes the rule of the game,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.300
and now the item that
you bought in the past,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.600
for hard money, isn't worth it anymore

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.810
because the properties of
the items have changed,

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:53.840
for instance.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.990
And actually there's a fun
story of Vitalik Buterin,

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.479
the founder of Ethereum,

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:01.203
actually once having
been said that he said

00:08:01.203 --> 00:08:04.070
that he was playing World of Warcraft,

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.180
the big and famous online game,

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.990
and he had some kind of character.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.570
And suddenly, Blizzard, the
creator of World of Warcraft

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.510
changed the rule, and now the character

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.570
wasn't as effective as it was before.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.780
And he spent literally
lots and lots of hours

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.740
into his character, in
order to make the character

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.640
as strong as it was, and he
really got angry about that.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.680
Which Vitalik says was one of the reasons

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.460
why he now created Ethereum.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.490
So I do not know if this
is actually true or not,

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.320
but I hope this provides a good example

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.933
of what is possible with storage
of assets on blockchains.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.187
In order to provide a few examples

00:08:38.187 --> 00:08:41.444
of projects that exist out
there that actually do that,

00:08:41.444 --> 00:08:43.527
one project is called Coins,

00:08:43.527 --> 00:08:47.820
it is a storage service,
stores asset metadata

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.570
to the Bitcoin blockchain,

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.421
and focuses on banking applications.

00:08:51.421 --> 00:08:53.973
And that's exactly what I
just talked about earlier,

00:08:53.973 --> 00:08:56.950
it's a way of implementing and using

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:00.103
the Colored Coin use case
that I just described.

00:09:00.987 --> 00:09:03.740
A second example in the
online gaming sphere

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.700
is Deckbound, it is a gaming platform

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:10.410
that you can create
strategic digital card games.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.551
And these cards that you're creating,

00:09:12.551 --> 00:09:14.240
they're stored to your account,

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.870
and the value of these cards is preserved.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:18.446
And this way it makes sure that

00:09:18.446 --> 00:09:20.290
there's no central institution

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.938
suddenly diminishing
the value of your cards.

00:09:22.938 --> 00:09:25.910
And finally, probably
the most famous example

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.190
on this page is CryptoKitties.

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:30.840
It was the first big
use case implementation

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.380
of the digital collectible use case

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.274
on the Ethereum blockchain.

00:09:35.274 --> 00:09:37.550
And the idea is that basically

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.680
by playing CryptoKitties
you can create these cats,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.047
and these cats are stored on a blockchain,

00:09:43.047 --> 00:09:44.860
and these cats have different value,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.220
depending on what
property these cats have.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.130
And then these cats can be traded

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.070
and exchanged with other people.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:52.903
So a form of a digital collectible.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.290
And in fact the CryptoKitties
was an extremely successful

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.603
example of such a use case.

00:09:59.603 --> 00:10:02.820
So this is a TechCrunch article

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.660
that was just written a few days

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:08.910
after the CryptoKitties use
case, or service, was launched.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.900
And it reports that within a few days

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.460
over one million US dollars
has been transacted.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.010
So kitties worth over
one million US dollars

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.310
have been bought, and they're
individual transactions

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:22.882
ranging from over $20,000 to over $100,000

00:10:22.882 --> 00:10:25.353
that were spent on individual Kitties.

00:10:25.353 --> 00:10:28.210
So of course, it's probably questionable

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:30.550
how sustainable that is, but this provides

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.900
a first understanding of
what might be possible,

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.870
and how relevant the use
case for digital collectibles

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.710
might become in the future.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:38.930
So, thank you for watching,

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.520
I hope you found this insightful,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.830
and now this helps you to
understand how businesses

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.779
can actually make use of the
new blockchain technology.

00:10:45.779 --> 00:10:50.130
This was now a deep dive on
the asset type of use cases,

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:51.332
but as I said in the beginning,

